## Instructions... (3436 chars)"
`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
1. **Struct-typed template data gets validation for free.** Go's `text/template` distinguishes structs from maps at execution time: a struct field lookup that doesn't exist (`{{.NotAField}}`) is always a hard error ("can't evaluate field..."), regardless of any `Option()` setting — that option only changes behavior for `map[string]any` lookups. By projecting `domain.MergeRequest` into a small named struct instead of a map, the "unknown variable" acceptance criterion was satisfied by the type system, with no bespoke validation code.

2. **Package placement followed the dependency graph, not intuition.** The bead flagged placement as undecided; tracing docs/architecture.md's import rules narrowed it to exactly one sane choice: of the packages that already import both `internal/domain` and `internal/config`, `internal/core` is a composition root (wrong layer for reusable logic) and `internal/tui` is where the ADR's `tea.ExecProcess` call will eventually live — so the pure builder sits right next to its future caller.

3. **Returning args-only instead of a combined argv slice mirrors the stdlib's own API shape.** `exec.Command(name string, arg ...string)` already splits binary from arguments; making `BuildCommandArgv` return just the resolved `[]string` args (not prepending `cmd.Binary`) means the next ticket calls `exec.Command(cmd.Binary, argv...)` with zero re-splitting logic.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

Implemented `BuildCommandArgv` in `internal/tui/command_argv.go`, resolving each `config.Command.Args` template entry against the ADR's seven-variable named projection via `text/template`. Added 11 table-driven subtests (all seven variables, a combined case, a literal arg, and unknown-variable/malformed-template error cases). `just check` passes; the package-placement rationale is now recorded as a "Decision" subsection in `docs/adr/0004-external-command-launcher.md`, and bead `mrr-4z4.3` is closed. Next up per the epic: `mrr-4z4.4`/`.5` (keybinding wiring and the actual `tea.ExecProcess` call).
